the united states says it's sharply  reducing its troop numbers in  afghanistan  and iraq by mid-january the deployment  will be cut  to two and a half thousand soldiers in  each country  president donald trump made a campaign  promise to end what he called  forever wars but the move has triggered  opposition both from leading republicans  and nato allies  the sun could be setting on the u.s  presence in afghanistan  american soldiers have been fighting  here since 2001  helping stabilize the country after  overthrowing taliban rule  but suffering over 2 000 deaths  over time troop numbers have waned now  outgoing president donald trump will  further reduce the number of soldiers  here  and in iraq in light of these tremendous  sacrifices  and with great humility and gratitude to  those who came before us i am formally  announcing  that we will implement president trump's  orders to continue our repositioning  of forces from those two countries by  january 15  2021 our forces their size in  afghanistan  will be two thousand five hundred troops  our force size in iraq will also  be two thousand five hundred as u.s  troop numbers dwindled extremists gained  in strength  this attack on kabul university last  week killed 22 people  it was claimed by the so-called islamic  state which has emerged as a rival to  the taliban  the taliban themselves have stepped up  their attacks on security forces and  civilians  violating an agreement that foresaw the  withdrawal of all u.s soldiers by may  next year criticism of the latest  drawdown  even came from within the president's  own party  i think it's extremely important here in  the next couple of months  not to have any earth shaking changes  with regard to defense and foreign  policy  i think a precipitous drawdown in either  afghanistan or iraq would be a mistake  in iraq the u.s military presence was  greatly reduced after the defeat of isis  in 2017  this has increased this way of  neighboring iran and militia groups  aligned with tehran  as u.s soldiers head home they could  risk leaving the field to america's  adversaries  let's go to brussels now and talk to our  correspondent terry schultz who has been  on the ground with nato troops in  afghanistan  terry i understand the head of nato has  expressed concern about the trump  administration's troop withdrawal plans  in iraq and afghanistan what more can  you tell us  that's right terry even before the  announcement was final secretary general  jens stolzenberg  put out a statement expressing the  closest that a norwegian diplomat comes  to being displeased he reminded the  united states  that the the presence in afghanistan is  conditions based  that means their precipitous withdrawal  right now when conditions are not being  met when they're still widespread  taliban violence  could mean leaving the country again  open to become  a terrorist haven secretary general  stoltenberg said  every country wants to bring its troops  home but the time is not now right  uh he also reminded the united states  that many nato soldiers  have fought and died alongside the  americans  has the trump administration terry  offered any strategic  justification for the drawdown in  afghanistan now  well it's not clear exactly what acting  defense secretary christopher miller  said  to secretary general stoltenberg in a  call they had on friday and  again a call earlier this week but  from press reports it seems that miller  did not exactly say to stoltenberg  that they were going to make this  announcement of the troop cuts  and this is really important because all  countries all of the the nato troops in  afghanistan  are to some extent reliant on  infrastructure provided by the united  states so this isn't really something  you want to leave  to a last-minute announcement what  effect  is this u.s drawdown in afghanistan  likely to have  terry on those other nato allies you  mentioned  yeah it's not like they can just pull  troops out of a sector where the united  states is  is deployed terry and that everyone else  will be fine  the united states was the first in it  provides a lot of this stability  in in which other troops operate not  just that but the  ngos civil society are all dependent on  the  security provided by these u.s troops  which are there both in a nato capacity  and  in a bilateral capacity there are there  are special operations forces no one  else has on the ground  so if this really happens and that  remains to be seen  um this will have a huge impact on nato  deployments and i'm sure that other  governments are having to look now at  what they're going to do with their own  presence on the ground  this is a very delicate time in  afghanistan with negotiations underway  between the taliban and the government  how could this draw down by u.s forces  change the stability of the situation in  afghanistan  well it certainly takes a lot of  pressure off the taliban  to negotiate and actually implement the  peace deal  that's now being discussed because as i  said before  the withdrawal was due to be conditions  based and that means that the taliban  needs to be living up to the peace deal  we have seen attacks continue even in  recent days even this week  and so if the troops pull out if the u.s  forces leave and they're followed by  other nato troops  the taliban doesn't have to comply with  this peace agreement  and as secretary general stoltenberg  warned they will have free reign of the  country once again  terry thank you very much for your  insights that was our correspondent  terry schultz in brussels
 

